WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.012
Detection logic depends on telemetry.

00:00:02.012 --> 00:00:06.217
An analytic can only evaluate behavior that the organization can observe,

00:00:06.217 --> 00:00:10.422
and its reliability depends on stable fields, understandable semantics, appropriate retention,

00:00:10.422 --> 00:00:13.098
and predictable delivery from the underlying sources.

00:00:13.098 --> 00:00:16.157
Detection planning should therefore begin with data requirements.

00:00:16.157 --> 00:00:20.362
Engineers identify the behavior to observe, determine which sources record it,

00:00:20.362 --> 00:00:24.950
confirm the necessary fields, and test how normal and suspicious scenarios appear.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:31.067
This prevents rules from being designed around data that does not exist or cannot be trusted.

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:33.360
Telemetry context also affects detection quality.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.419
Asset criticality, identity role, device state, vulnerability information,

00:00:36.419 --> 00:00:41.389
and prior activity can help a detection distinguish routine behavior from meaningful risk.

00:00:41.389 --> 00:00:47.123
Poor context may create noise or leave analysts without enough information to triage an alert.

00:00:47.123 --> 00:00:50.182
Detection and telemetry teams need a feedback loop.

00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:54.387
When fields change, sources stop reporting, or analysts identify missing context,

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:55.916
detections should be reviewed.

00:00:55.916 --> 00:00:56.566
Likewise,

00:00:56.566 --> 00:01:01.536
detection failures can reveal collection gaps that deserve engineering attention and coverage investment.

00:01:01.536 --> 00:01:06.124
Testing should cover the data pipeline as well as the analytic rule.

00:01:06.124 --> 00:01:11.094
Teams can generate an approved test event, confirm that the source records it,

00:01:11.094 --> 00:01:14.535
verify that collection and parsing preserve the expected fields,

00:01:14.535 --> 00:01:17.975
and then check whether the detection produces useful context.

00:01:17.975 --> 00:01:23.060
This end-to-end method separates logic problems from telemetry failures and supports safer change management.
